[x] Publish the specification at clearhead.dev =spec-site $Decision 49. Add book.toml and SUMMARY.md over the existing spec Markdown (mdbook 0.5 is installed) and a _redirects file: /vocab/app/v1 to the vocabulary's page, /vocab/app/v1/:version and /schemas/:version/:name to the tagged release. The human creates the Cloudflare Pages project, authorizes its GitHub app on the specifications repo, sets the production branch to release and the build to mdbook build, and points clearhead.dev at it. Also confirm the domain auto-renews (registered 2026-10-03 for one year). DONE 2026-10-05: specifications' build-site builds the mdbook with a pinned, checksummed mdbook (Cloudflare's build image has none, so the build command is sh build-site, output book) and _redirects resolves the names. The human's dashboard setup made a Worker with static assets named specifications, the dashboard's default, not a Pages project; clearhead.dev and clearhead.us both point at it. The old clearhead-ontology Pages project (wired to the folded ontology repo, every build failed) is deleted. The human moved the domains to a current card, which revived both; auto-renew is not readable with the wrangler login and is for the human to confirm in the registrar.$ !2 ^2026-10-02T23:45 %2026-10-05T11:04 #01a10083-25b6-758b-9c92-7c9a704f3088
[x] Release the specification as v0.2.0 with a release branch =spec-release-0-2 $Decision 49. One release commit: schema \$ids move to https://clearhead.dev/schemas/v0.2.0/<name>.schema.json, the changelog's 2026-10-02 entries become v0.2.0. Tag v0.2.0, create the release branch at the tag; Cloudflare Pages then publishes. Proof: https://clearhead.dev/vocab/app/v1 and a schema \$id resolve. DONE 2026-10-05: v0.2.0 is tagged on main and release is at the tag, both pushed; clearhead-core's spec pin stamps the v0.2.0 URLs. Proof held via public DNS and on the human's phone: /vocab/app/v1 302s to /ontology#application-graph (200), and /schemas/v0.2.0/actions.schema.json 302s to the tagged file (200), whose \$id is that URL.$ !2 ^2026-10-02T23:45 %2026-10-05T11:04 <migrate-iris <spec-site #01a10083-25c7-74d5-a73b-438671a3c4c2
[x] Publish the grammar 0.10.0 to crates.io =publish-grammar $Decided with the human 2026-10-05: the CLI ships as prebuilt binaries installable with cargo binstall, and binstall reads the crate from crates.io, which refuses a published crate with a git dependency. crates.io has tree-sitter-actions 0.9.4; Core and the CLI pin 0.10.0 by git rev. Publish 0.10.0 (cargo package --list first: the generated parser and bindings, nothing the build does not need), then depend on it by version in both crates. Open: how the pinned platform composition keeps building against the grammar submodule once the dependency is a registry version; a \[patch.crates-io] in the composition is the usual answer. DONE 2026-10-05: 0.10.0 is on crates.io (tag v0.10.0); the crate now ships the MIT license and not node_modules' licenses, as 0.9.4 did. Decided with the human (option A): Core takes the published grammar in every checkout, as a consumer does, so the platform .cargo/config.toml patch to the submodule is gone. That patch had also made Core's committed lockfile name a local path, failing --locked in Core's CI for at least its last 100 runs. validate-pinned proves the published grammar Core resolves is the submodule, file for file; an unpublished grammar change uses a one-invocation --config override (Core's README). Core's --no-default-features tests that format .actions are now gated on the formatting feature.$ !2 ^2026-10-05T19:31 %2026-10-05T20:14 #01a10f0d-78f9-7235-8c70-651a9144080c
[x] Name the implemented spec version in clearhead --version =version-names-spec $Decided 2026-10-05: each repository has its own semver, so the CLI says which specification release it implements, for example clearhead 0.3.0 (spec v0.2.0). Derive it at build time, never hand-typed, so it cannot drift. SOURCE (2026-10-05): clearhead_core's \[package.metadata.clearhead] specification, which ships with the published crate; Core's CI tests against that tag and validate-pinned requires the spec submodule to be exactly it, so --version reads the one declaration the tests already prove. DONE 2026-10-06: Core's build.rs reads the declaration (toml, a build dependency only) into clearhead_core::SPECIFICATION; clearhead --version prints clearhead 0.3.0 (specification v0.2.0), tested in tests/version.rs. The command is now named clearhead, not clearhead_cli.$ !3 ^2026-10-05T19:31 %2026-10-06T15:09 #01a10f0d-791b-752b-ad33-03e365266965
[x] Publish clearhead_core to crates.io =publish-core $crates.io has clearhead_core 0.2.0 from 2026-04-12. The API has changed since, so bump by semver (0.3.0), cargo publish --dry-run first, and check the wasm32 target dependencies and features package cleanly. Versions are independent per repository (decided 2026-10-05). DONE 2026-10-06: clearhead_core 0.3.0 is on crates.io, with the MIT license (a symlink Cargo packages by content) and its specification declaration in the published manifest.$ !2 ^2026-10-05T19:31 %2026-10-06T15:09 <publish-grammar #01a10f0d-790b-7001-b625-193df342be41
[x] Build CLI release binaries with dist =cli-dist $dist (formerly cargo-dist, v0.33) declares targets in Cargo.toml and generates a GitHub workflow that builds both binaries on a tag and attaches archives named as binstall expects. First fix clearhead_cli repository: it names ClearHeadToDo-Devs/clearhead_cli.git, which is a 404, and binstall looks for release assets there. DECIDED 2026-10-06 (human: simplicity is a virtue of its own): one target, x86_64 Linux, and one build per target, the full default (sparql on) with both binaries, clearhead and clearhead-lsp. binstall resolves one artifact per crate, version and target, so feature variants would each need their own crate, for 3.2 MB (16.8 vs 13.6 MB release). Minimal is a source build, documented in the README: cargo install clearhead_cli --no-default-features. If the LSP is ever wanted alone, split it by binary into its own package, not by feature. Releases follow user-visible change, not a calendar (decided 2026-10-05). DONE 2026-10-06: dist 0.33.0 (dist-workspace.toml, release.yml, profile.dist) builds one x86_64 Linux archive per version tag with both binaries, LICENSE, README and CHANGELOG, plus its sha256; no installer scripts. The repository URL is fixed. The CI-built binary needs glibc 2.34, so Debian bookworm and Ubuntu 22.04 run it too.$ !2 ^2026-10-05T19:31 %2026-10-06T15:09 <publish-core #01a10f0d-7929-703a-88e3-7a17a557697d
[x] Release the CLI so cargo binstall downloads it =cli-release $crates.io has clearhead_cli 0.2.1 from 2025-12-24, so cargo binstall clearhead_cli builds that old version from source today. Bump to 0.3.0, tag, let the dist workflow publish the binaries, then cargo publish. Proof: on a machine without a Rust toolchain, cargo binstall clearhead_cli downloads rather than compiles (its log names the strategy), and clearhead --version shows 0.3.0 and the spec release. DONE 2026-10-06: v0.3.0 tagged on clearhead-core, the release workflow attached the archive (https://github.com/ClearHeadToDo-Devs/clearhead-core/releases/tag/v0.3.0), and clearhead_cli 0.3.0 is on crates.io. Proof: cargo binstall with compile and quick-install disabled downloaded it from github.com in 2.3 s, and it reports clearhead 0.3.0 (specification v0.2.0).$ !2 ^2026-10-05T19:31 %2026-10-06T15:09 <cli-dist <version-names-spec #01a10f0d-793a-77ed-9421-68448777e645
[_] Give CLI releases their changelog as release notes =release-notes $Found 2026-10-06: dist found no notes for v0.3.0 in crates/clearhead-cli/CHANGELOG.md (a 0.3.0 heading exists), even through a root symlink; the dated and Roadmap headings are the likely cause. The archive ships the changelog regardless.$ !5 ^2026-10-06T15:09 %2026-10-06T15:50 #01a11343-bb8c-74f1-a661-76c8ed3ad8a0
[x] Rename the CLI crate to clearhead =rename-cli-crate $Leaning yes with the human, 2026-10-06: the crate takes its command's name, so cargo binstall clearhead and cargo install clearhead work. The name is unclaimed on crates.io today; the old README told people to install it, so it should not stay open long. At the next CLI release: rename the package in crates/clearhead-cli/Cargo.toml (keep the bins), publish clearhead, update the README install section and the archive name dist derives (clearhead-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.xz), and publish a last clearhead_cli whose README says it moved. DONE 2026-10-06 as 0.3.1 (a small release, the human's call): clearhead is on crates.io and v0.3.1 ships clearhead-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.xz; the library keeps the name clearhead_cli. No moved notice was needed: clearhead_cli 0.3.0's README already says cargo install clearhead.$ !3 ^2026-10-06T15:53 %2026-10-06T16:06 #01a1136c-7838-77c9-82ae-09c7b0e13c64
